# XSH raise()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.raise.001
anchor     functions/raise.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       raise(SIGUSR1) with a handler returns 0, after the handler has run
           and returned.
note       raise() came from ISO C (Issue 4); 2001 is the first POSIX.1
           edition with it. Its rule that it does not return before a
           handler it causes returns makes delivery before return
           required (see Q-11).
test       t/001-handler-before-return.c

assertion  XSH.raise.002
anchor     functions/raise.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       raise() sends the signal to the calling thread: while another
           thread with the signal unblocked exists, raise() in a thread
           that has it unblocked runs the handler in that thread, and in a
           thread that has it blocked leaves it pending for that thread
           until it unblocks it.
test       t/002-calling-thread.c

assertion  XSH.raise.003
anchor     functions/raise.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       raise() with signal number sysconf(_SC_NSIG) (one above the highest
           supported) returns a non-zero value and sets errno to EINVAL.
test       t/003-einval.c
